tonight our series on the state of the  metropolitan police department continues  while there have been calls for police  reform one major change that took place  at metro involves the banning  of a controversial restraint by officers  called a lateral  vascular neck restraint the department  took action in july  to limit its use and then during a  special session nevada legislators  banned it  the i-team's vanessa murphy takes a  broader look at a number of use of force  incidents at the hands of metro officers  the department of justice announced in  2012 the metro police department needed  to change  after an extensive review the doj wrote  in this report  the shooting of unarmed suspects remains  a concern  for example between 2007 and 2011 there  were 10  officer involved shootings that involved  unarmed suspects  seven were black i think it's safe to  say they were not in keeping  with best practices the department  implemented multiple changes  in 2019 metro reports out of 16  shootings by police  15 of the subjects were armed there is  an  emphasis on de-escalation techniques  in this report analyzing a five-year  period through 2019  the department provides use of force  data while also acknowledging  lessons are still being learned it notes  four critical incidents in 2019  in which the coroner determined officers  actions  may have contributed to the subject's  cause of death  [Music]  the clark county coroner's office ruled  byron williams death a homicide because  the prone restraint officers placed him  in was a factor  in addition to meth in his system and  health issues  he ran from police after they tried  stopping him for not having a bicycle  light  once they had him in custody williams  told officers he couldn't breathe at  least 24  times multiple officers deactivated  their body cameras  at the scene he deserves justice like  what they did to him  was not was not right it was not fair it  was inhumane  the use of force review board comprised  of both commissioned officers and three  citizens  determined officers should have  immediately rolled williams on his side  after he was handcuffed  and their medical response violated  training and policy  metro took action notifying all officers  to place suspects on their side or sit  them up  so they are not kept in a position like  williams was  and to get medical attention immediately  in a situation like this  the department also updated policy  officers must keep their body cameras on  while at a scene  donovan britt is a citizen volunteer on  the review board we kind of see  these events and we understand what they  should be doing and something that's  outside of the norm we have  we can focus in on and go why is that  the case the board also makes  recommendations on officer discipline  according to the department out of 31  cases reviewed in 2019  14 officers were disciplined none were  fired  and one resigned captain fred haas works  in internal affairs  if there are bad cops out there i don't  want them standing next to me out there  in the community  and throughout the community in 2020  there were black lives matter protests  and calls for police reform while metro  is more advanced than many departments  incidents like william's death  demonstrate there are still  serious flaws that need to be fixed  vanessa murphy 8 news now we also have  more information on police use of force  hirings and firings and diversity on the  force on 8newsnow.com  click on the state of metro banner  you'll find that right at the top of the  page
 

